{"benchmark": "ifeval", "task_id": "1000", "sample_idx": 28, "model_name": "qwen/qwen3.5-122b-a10b", "content": "Raymond III was a prominent nobleman who served as the Count of Tripoli during the twelfth century. He held this position for nearly forty years and played a critical role in the politics of the Crusader States. His life began with a period of captivity under Nur ad-Din which lasted for many years before his release allowed him to reclaim his lands. This long imprisonment shaped his political approach and forced him to navigate complex alliances within the region. He eventually became one of the most powerful figures in the Levant due to his strategic location and diplomatic skills.\n\n### Early Life and Captivity\nRaymond inherited the County of Tripoli as a minor after the death of his father. The situation required careful management because his youth made him vulnerable to powerful neighbors. Nur ad-Din captured the young count and held him in Damascus for sixteen years. This extended period in captivity prevented him from ruling his own domains directly during those years. His mother acted as regent while he remained a prisoner. The release finally came through negotiations that involved significant ransom payments and political concessions. This experience taught him the value of patience and caution in his dealings with Muslim rulers.\n\n### Political Influence and Regency\nUpon his return Raymond found the Kingdom of Jerusalem in a state of flux following the death of King Baldwin IV. He became a key advisor and eventually served as regent for the young Baldwin V. His influence grew as he balanced the interests of various factions within the Latin East. He often advocated for a policy of détente with Muslim powers to preserve the fragile Christian territories. This stance put him at odds with more militant barons who favored constant warfare. His ability to maintain stability during a time of crisis demonstrated his political acumen.\n\n### Declin